// C++14/11 Senior Engineer Rules for Legacy Codebases

# C++ (2011/2014) Best Practices:

- Restrict code and dependencies to features available in C++11 and C++14; avoid C++17 and beyond.
- All dynamically allocated resources must use smart pointers (`std::unique_ptr`, `std::shared_ptr`, favor `std::make_unique` (C++14)); avoid raw `new`/`delete`.
- Always use `nullptr` in place of `NULL` or `0` for pointer values.
- Use `const` and `constexpr` wherever possible for safety and clarity.
- Iterators: Prefer `auto` for non-business logic types, but keep explicit types for basic types and business logic clarity.
- No use of `std::string_view`, `std::optional`, or `std::filesystem`. Stick to `std::string`, raw pointers, or project-provided alternatives.
- Use RAII for manual resource guards (custom guards if necessary, since `std::scope_exit` doesn't exist).
- Legacy pointer ownership: Carefully audit all raw pointer usages and check for `nullptr` before dereferencing.
- Memory: Use `std::move` and `std::forward` for container/string ownership transfers; pass large objects by `const&`.
- Header guards should be `#pragma once` in new or modified headers.
- Follow the existing naming convention (snake_case/camelCase) in the touched code.
- Minimize large-scale refactoring; focus on local, targeted changes.
- If project builds with CMake, use target-based commands (`target_link_libraries`, etc.) and remind to update relevant `CMakeLists.txt` when adding sources. Use `set_target_properties(target PROPERTIES CXX_STANDARD 14)` when generating new targets.
- For loops, use pre-increment (`++it`) for iterators.
- Clean up unused headers if files are edited.
- No unnecessary theory comments or excessive code explanations—only note *why* something is needed if it deviates from the prevailing pattern or for safety/compatibility.
